ch-1-blog"}>{"Blog"}</a></li><li className={"breadcrumb-item"} aria-current={"page"}>{"How to Quit a Job You're Good At"}</li></ol></nav><h1 id={"lc-ph-title"} className={"max-w-[13ch] text-[clamp(2.75rem,1.5rem_+_3.9vw,5.5rem)] font-normal leading-[1.03] tracking-[-0.02em] text-balance custom-ph-title"} data-anim={"fade-up"} data-anim-delay={"80"} data-anim-duration={"1200"}>{"How to quit a job "}<em>{"you're good at."}</em></h1><p className={"max-w-128 mt-6 text-[clamp(1.125rem,1.02rem_+_0.35vw,1.3125rem)] leading-[1.6] text-pretty custom-ph-lead"} data-anim={"fade-up"} data-anim-delay={"200"}>{"Being good at something is the strongest handcuff there is. Here's how to loosen it slowly, and leave well."}</p></div></div></section><article id={"lc-article"} className={"custom-section"} aria-labelledby={"lc-ph-title"}><div className={"container"}><div className={"max-w-256 mx-auto flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-y-4 gap-x-8 mb-7 custom-post-byline"} data-anim={"fade-up"}><p className={"custom-post-author"}><img src={"https://draftmango.com/framo/assets/media/placeholder/1350x1800.svg"} alt={""} width={"1350"} height={"1800"} decoding={"async"} /><span>{"Isla Bennett"}<small>{"12 Oct 2026"}</small></span></p><p className={"custom-blog-meta"}><span className={"custom-blog-topic"}>{"Career Change"}</span><span>{"7 min read"}</span></p></div><figure className={"max-w-256 mx-auto overflow-clip aspect-video rounded-(--custom-radius-photo) bg-(--custom-tint) custom-post-cover"} data-anim={"fade"}><img src={"https://draftmango.com/framo/assets/media/placeholder/1800x1200.svg"} alt={"A man in a camel jumper writing at a small desk, headphones round his neck"} width={"1800"} height={"1200"} fetchPriority={"high"} decoding={"async"} /></figure></div><div className={"article-body [--article-w:42rem] mt-[clamp(3rem,2rem_+_3vw,4.5rem)] custom-post-body"}><p className={"text-[clamp(1.25rem,1.15rem_+_0.4vw,1.4375rem)] leading-[1.55] text-text"}>{"Callum was the best analyst on his team. He was also quietly miserable. When he first came to me, he said something I hear every month: “If I were bad at it, leaving would be easy.”"}</p><p>{"He was right. Being good at a job you've outgrown is its own kind of trap. The praise keeps coming, the pay keeps rising, and every review makes leaving look a little more foolish. So you stay another year, and then another."}</p><h2>{"Why good is harder to leave than bad"}</h2><p>{"When a job is going badly, the decision makes itself. When it's going well, you have to choose against evidence, and most of us aren't built for that. Three things usually hold people in place:"}</p><ul><li><strong>{"Identity."}</strong>{" “I'm the person who's great at this.” Leaving feels like losing a piece of yourself."}</li><li><strong>{"Sunk cost."}</strong>{" Ten years of training, and a voice that says you'd be wasting it."}</li><li><strong>{"Other people's pride."}</strong>{" A parent, a partner or a manager who loves the version of you that stays."}</li></ul><p>{"None of these are reasons to stay. They're reasons to go slowly."}</p><blockquote className={"custom-post-quote"}><p>{"You're not wasting ten years. You're taking them "}<em>{"with you."}</em></p></blockquote><h2>{"Test before you leap"}</h2><p>{"The biggest mistake I see is the dramatic exit: a resignation on a Friday and a plan on the Monday. The people who change well run small experiments first, while the salary still arrives."}</p><ol><li><strong>{"Talk to three people"}</strong>{" who already do the work you're curious about. Ask what a dull Tuesday looks like."}</li><li><strong>{"Do a small piece of it"}</strong>{" on evenings or weekends: a short course, a volunteer shift, one paid project."}</li><li><strong>{"Notice your energy"}</strong>{" afterwards, not just your interest. Tired and happy is a good sign."}</li></ol><aside className={"custom-post-try"} aria-labelledby={"lc-article-try"}><span className={"custom-sun custom-sun--accent bottom-0 end-8 -z-1 w-24"} data-anim={""} aria-hidden={"true"}></span><p className={"flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.9375rem] font-semibold text-primary-ink custom-post-try-label"} id={"lc-article-try"}><i className={"ph ph-pencil-simple-line"} aria-hidden={"true"}></i>{"Try this · 10 minutes"}</p><p className={"custom-post-try-title"}>{"The two-column week"}</p><p>{"For one working week, keep a page with two columns. On the left, write down every task that gave you energy. On the right, every one that drained it. On Friday, circle the three that surprised you."}</p><p>{"You're not looking for a new career on that page. You're looking for a direction."}</p></aside><h2>{"Build a runway, not a cliff"}</h2><p>{"Money is the fear behind most of the other fears, so name it. Work out the lowest monthly figure you could live on for six months, then decide how many of those months you want saved before you go. For most of my clients, that number is smaller than they feared."}</p><figure><img src={"https://draftmango.com/framo/assets/media/placeholder/1600x1067.svg"} alt={"A sealed cream envelope and a pen beside a closed laptop and a cup of coffee on a pale oak desk"} width={"1600"} height={"1067"} loading={"lazy"} decoding={"async"} /><figcaption>{"The day the email goes is quieter than you expect. The work you did before it is what counts."}</figcaption></figure><h2>{"Leave well"}</h2><p>{"You're good at this job, so leave like it. Give generous notice, hand over properly, and thank the people who taught you. The industry you're leaving is smaller than you think, and the bridge you keep may be the one you need."}</p><p>{"Callum left eight months after our first session, for a junior developer role and a pay cut he'd planned for. He still says the hardest part wasn't the leaving. It was believing he was allowed to."}</p></div></article><section id={"lc-article-author"} className={"custom-section"} aria-label={"About the author"}><div className={"container"}><div className={"grid